  {
   "id": "wallet-backend#803",
   "pipeline": "wallet-backend",
   "fix": "ff73ff46",
   "parent": "e967ce8b",
   "title": "validate Blend pool name bytes and length on decode",
   "verdict": "detected",
   "entries": [
    {
     "entry": "blend-pool-name",
     "results": "sequences",
     "blind": false,
     "parent": {
      "indexer: mishandled": 1,
      "unexplained": 1
     },
     "fix": {}
    }
   ],
   "condition": "Stable: the parent failed 5 of 5 runs and the fix passed 5 of 5, on both inputs. The pool is valid network input: deployed directly, its constructor authorized by its admin, with a name holding a NUL byte, which a Soroban String allows. The pool runs Blend's own wasm: pool v2, byte for byte the one uploaded on pubnet in ledger 56,615,381 (sha256 a41fc53d), which the scenario takes from Stellar's public ledger archive; stellar-core 30.0.0 accepted the deploy and wallet-backend classified the pool by its own signature check, with Blend production on (README, \"Protocol production\"). At the parent the ledger stops on \"invalid byte sequence for encoding UTF8: 0x00\" upserting blend pools, as the fix's description says.",
   "what_would_catch_it": null
  },
  {
   "id": "wallet-backend#802",
   "pipeline": "wallet-backend",
   "fix": "3e22515a",
   "parent": "ff73ff46",
   "title": "cap Blend decimals at 70",
   "verdict": "missed",
   "entries": [],
   "what_would_catch_it": "What the remaining Blend miss needs. Protocol production runs in replays (`--protocols`, README \"Protocol production\"), and Blend's real pool wasm is in the corpus (`localcore-blend-upload-3`, from pubnet ledger 56,615,381), so a pool can be deployed and classified as for `ff73ff46`. Then, for this fix: a reserve with decimals above 70 set on that pool through its admin calls (queue_set_reserve, then set_reserve), which Blend's own code validates; not built yet."
  },
